text:
maria: heads up - hitting a weird TS + edge bundler issue in the new sample app. generics collapse to `any` after bundling, editors show correct types but runtime shapes miss keys. repro in samples/edge-adapter.
kevin: can you paste the tiny repro and tsconfig? quick triage helps.
maria: minimal handler below:
```ts
// samples/edge-adapter/src/handler.ts
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
export default async function handler(req: Request) {
  const r = await client.embed<string[]>({ input: 'ok' });
  // Editor shows string[], but after bundle `r` is any[] and runtime logs show objects
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ out: r[0] }));
}
```
tsconfig used by the sample:
```json
{ "compilerOptions": { "target": "ES2020", "module": "ESNext", "moduleResolution": "bundler", "isolatedModules": true, "declaration": true, "esModuleInterop": false } }
```
maria: build output from esbuild: "warning: Could not parse declaration files emitted by TypeScript. Falling back to any." and deploy runtime: "TypeError: createClient is not a function" in edge route.
jon: likely two problems: 1) our exported types relied on several overloads with no single implementation signature which breaks `isolatedModules`/bundlers; 2) CJS/ESM interop differences make default/named import ambiguous when esModuleInterop is false.
kevin: options: A) collapse outward-facing overloads to a single generic-based signature to keep inference stable; B) ship a small `index.d.ts` shim that re-exports a canonical signature so bundlers pick it up; C) update sample docs to recommend `esModuleInterop: true` or `module: 'NodeNext'` for modern runtimes.
sophie: i'm +1 for A + B. Overloads are nice but brittle across targets. we should keep internal helpers but export one stable signature. sketch: `export function generate<T = string | Record<string, unknown>>(opts: GenerateOptions): Promise<T>;` and `export function embed<T = unknown>(opts: { input: string | string[] } & EmbedOptions): Promise<T[]>;`.
sam: also add package.json `types` pointing to a single d.ts entry and a `types/compat.d.ts` for legacy patterns. that reduces ambiguous deep-import picks.
kevin: CI change request: matrix job to typecheck with `isolatedModules` + run esbuild bundling (edge target) + a simple CJS/ESM import smoke test. block on failures.
maria: CI just failed for my sample PR: https://ci.internal/redsdk/4982 - types + runtime mismatch. ci-bot assigned @sophie.
ci-bot: Build failed - see https://ci.internal/redsdk/4982 (typecheck, tests). @sophie assigned.
sophie: i'll open a PR that: 1) replaces public overloads with single generic signatures; 2) adds `types/compat.d.ts` and `dist/index.d.ts` shim; 3) updates package.json `types` field; 4) updates samples/edge-adapter/README.md with recommended tsconfig and a short troubleshooting section about esModuleInterop and isolatedModules; 5) adds CI matrix job for tsc + esbuild.
jon: after the change we should smoke-run a few internal prod routes that use deep generic shapes. i'll queue that.
kevin: also add a short migration note in RELEASE_NOTES / BREAKING.md so customers relying on overload inference can pin or follow migration steps.
sophie: PR opened: #2783 - tagging @kevin @jon for review. i'll add a tiny runnable repro under samples/edge-adapter so contributors can test locally with `esbuild --bundle`.
maria: thanks all - appreciate the quick triage. this will help onboarding docs and reduce support noise. :thumbsup:
